<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I'm at School – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Places I Can Go</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I'm at School.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write simple phrases and sentences on familiar topics. Interactive: Write short role plays to be acted out based on class content.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write simple phrases and sentences about places like home and school (e.g., "I'm at home."). Can write and perform, based on class content, short role plays about places (e.g., "Where are you?" "I'm at school."). Can write simple captions for classroom images and content (e.g., write "This is the library." for a picture of the library).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of different places (school, market, park, home, library, cafeteria, gym). Students orally review the names of the places. Teacher asks, "Where am I?" as they point to a flashcard, and students respond, "You're at the [place]." This activates prior knowledge of vocabulary and the "I'm at..." structure.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes two simple sentences on the board: "I'm at school." and "This is the library." The teacher points to each word as they write, saying them aloud. They emphasize the use of contractions ("I'm") and prepositions of place ("at"). The teacher then shows a picture of the playground and writes "This is the playground. I can play here." on the board, modeling the process of describing a place in writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I writing about food or a place?" (Expected answer: A place.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I use 'at' or 'under' for 'school' in 'I'm at school'?" (Expected answer: At.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Start Time]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Places I Can Go".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple poster or whiteboard display with 4-5 pictures of familiar places (school, home, park, library) with simple written captions below them. For example: <br>
                1. Picture of a school: "This is a school. I'm at school. It's big."<br>
                2. Picture of a home: "This is my home. It's clean."<br>
                3. Picture of a park: "This is the park. I play at the park."<br>
                4. Picture of a library: "This is the library. I read at the library. It's quiet."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher reads each caption aloud, pointing to the words, then has students chorally repeat. Attention is drawn to the /sk/ sound in "school" and the /m/ sound in "market" if the word is present, reinforcing phonemic awareness through reading the captions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students understand simple written descriptions of familiar places and can identify key information such as the name of the place, its location, and simple characteristics or activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to a caption and asks, "Where is this place?" Students point to the corresponding picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False questions about the captions, e.g., "Is the library quiet?" (Students give a thumbs up/down for Yes/No).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing simple sentences using contractions (I'm), prepositions of place (at, in, next to), and target vocabulary (home, school, library, park, cafeteria, playground).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with sentences that have blanks. Students choose words from a word bank (e.g., at, school, library, home, play, quiet) to complete the sentences. <br>
                        Examples: <br>
                        "I'm ___ (at/in) ___ (school/home)." <br>
                        "This is the ___ (library/park). It's ___ (quiet/big)." <br>
                        "I ___ (play/read) in the ___ (playground/market)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Picture Captioning):</b> The teacher shows 3-4 different pictures of school places (e.g., cafeteria, playground, nurse's office, principal's office). For each picture, students are given a sentence starter (e.g., "This is the...", "I'm at the...") and must complete the sentence and add one more simple sentence to describe an activity or characteristic. <br>
                        Example for Cafeteria picture: "This is the cafeteria. I eat lunch here."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Checking:</b> Students swap their completed worksheets/captions with a partner. They check each other's work for correct vocabulary and simple sentence structure (e.g., "Did you write 'at' for the place? Is the place name correct?"). The teacher circulates, providing support and correction as needed.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Places I Can Go".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Guided short role-play writing and simple scene description. Students will create short written pieces that can be acted out or presented as captions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher divides students into pairs. Each pair receives a set of 2-3 picture cards showing different places (e.g., school, park, market, home, library).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For each picture, students must write two things:
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A simple caption describing the place using "This is the..." and "It's [adjective]." (e.g., "This is the market. It's busy.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A very short dialogue (2 lines) between two characters asking and confirming location. <br>
                                Example: <br>
                                Character A: "Where are you?" <br>
                                Character B: "I'm at school."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Encourage the use of polite expressions, for example, if one student writes "Your home is beautiful," the other should write "Thank you!"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work collaboratively to write their captions and dialogues, focusing on familiar places from the theme "I'm at School" and "Places I Can Go". The teacher provides sentence frames on the board for support (e.g., "Where are you?", "I'm at ____.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After writing, pairs can briefly 'perform' their short dialogues by reading them aloud, pointing to their written work.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and collection of written work for review. The teacher uses a simple checklist to note students' ability to form basic sentences and use target vocabulary/grammar in writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their written captions and short dialogues. The teacher reviews them for the following criteria.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Correctly uses names of places (e.g., school, library, home, park) and verbs (e.g., play, read, go).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Correct use of "I'm at..." and other simple present tense forms. Correct use of prepositions of place (at, in, next to).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Cohesion:</b> Sentences are understandable and relate to the given pictures/scenario.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mechanics:</b> Basic capitalization for sentences and proper nouns, and end punctuation (period, question mark).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sociolinguistic:</b> Incorporates polite expressions where appropriate in the dialogue.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I learned to write about ______."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I used in my writing is ______."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing about places was the most fun for you today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where can you use these sentences about places outside of class?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in engaging students in writing tasks? The guided role-play writing seemed to encourage participation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students still struggle with basic sentence formation (subject-verb agreement). More structured sentence frames or individual whiteboards for quick corrections could help. Need to reinforce correct spelling of key vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I further encourage creativity within the writing constraints for this level?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will use their written captions and dialogues to create a small "Places Around My School" poster or a class book, integrating their writing with visual elements for a final project, and practicing presenting their work.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>